    <!-- Risks -->
    <section id="risks" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">The Race to the Bottom: Buyer Risks</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        When margins approach zero, factories do not advertise the savings they make — they engineer them in. These are the four most common down-specifications in cluster orders, and how to detect them:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Wire diameter shaving.</strong> Contract says 1.0 mm wire, you receive 0.93 mm. On a 10-ton welded mesh order, 0.07 mm of missing steel saves the factory roughly a ton of raw material. Detect it with a micrometer (0.01 mm resolution) on multiple points of multiple rolls — never accept a single measurement.</li>
        <li><strong>Zinc coating thinning.</strong> ASTM A641 Class 3 wire carries about 120 g/m² of zinc; Class 1 roughly 60 g/m²; some cluster wire tests below 40 g/m² while being sold as "heavy galvanized." Detect it with a magnetic coating-thickness gauge, or weigh a measured length of wire against its theoretical steel weight — the excess is zinc. Even at 245 g/m², zinc adds only about 3% to wire weight, so scale-based detection needs a decent sample size.</li>
        <li><strong>Steel grade substitution.</strong> 201-series stainless sold as 304, or Q195 mild steel passed off as higher-tensile wire for gabions. Detect it with a handheld XRF analyzer (most labs and many inspection agencies offer it for a small fee) and a magnet test as a rough screen.</li>
        <li><strong>Open-area gaming.</strong> For woven mesh, a slightly thinner wire or looser weave raises open area and lowers material cost while appearing to meet a mesh-count spec. Detect it by weighing a known area of the sample and comparing with theoretical weight — our <a href="/tools/woven-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">woven mesh weight calculator</a> and <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded mesh weight calculator</a> give you the reference number in seconds.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">The 15% Rule</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          In our experience comparing cluster quotes, a bid more than about 15% below the median of 4–5 comparable quotes is not a bargain — it is a warning. Legitimate low quotes exist (spare line capacity, existing raw stock), but they should still land within roughly 15% of the pack. Beyond that, ask the bidder in writing to state wire diameter, zinc class, and steel grade explicitly; evasive answers tell you everything.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Leverage -->
    <section id="leverage" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Turning Competition Into Leverage: The Multi-Quote Strategy</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Because the cluster is so dense, no single factory has pricing power over you — but only if the factories know they are competing. The multi-quote strategy converts 内卷 from a quality risk into a negotiating asset:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Send one identical RFQ to 3–5 factories.</strong> Use the <a href="/tools/inquiry-template-generator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">inquiry template generator</a> so every spec — material grade, wire diameter with tolerance, mesh size, coating class, dimensions, packaging — is identical. Different RFQs produce incomparable quotes.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote a realistic annual volume, not your first order.</strong> Cluster factories price on volume expectations. State expected annual offtake and ask for tiered pricing at 1×, 3×, and 5× your trial quantity.</li>
        <li><strong>Disclose that you are comparing multiple quotes.</strong> Naming 3–5 is enough; naming ten triggers "this buyer will never order" dismissal. Cluster sellers respond to credible competition with their real price, not list price.</li>
        <li><strong>Play the service card explicitly.</strong> When two quotes are within a few percent, ask each factory to compete on extras: free inspection photos, upgraded export pallets, a retained golden sample, faster lead time. In a race-to-zero-margin market, service is where factories differentiate — and much of it costs them little.</li>
        <li><strong>Never bluff on switching.</strong> Cluster networks are tight; salespeople talk. Use only real, documented competing quotes.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Multi-quoting works best when your shortlist contains real manufacturers rather than trading companies reselling the same workshop's output. Our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">verified factory directory</a> lists pre-screened Anping manufacturers by product category so your "competition" is genuine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Normalized comparison -->
    <section id="normalized" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Weight-Normalized Quote Comparison</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The single most powerful technique for buying in a race-to-the-bottom market: convert every quote to price per kilogram of finished mesh, then compare against your independently calculated theoretical weight. A factory cannot hide wire shaving or zinc thinning from a scale. Worked example — 10,000 m² of galvanized welded mesh, 50 × 50 mm openings, 2.5 mm wire, theoretical weight about 1.3 kg/m² (compute it with the <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded mesh weight calculator</a>). Three cluster quotes come in:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Quote</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">FOB Unit Price</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Stated Weight</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Implied $/kg</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Verdict</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Factory A</td>
              <td class="p-3">$1.75/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.30 kg/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">$1.35/kg</td>
              <td class="p-3">Baseline</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Factory B</td>
              <td class="p-3">$1.58/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.16 kg/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">$1.36/kg</td>
              <td class="p-3">Cheapest per m² — but weight is 11% low; likely 2.2–2.3 mm wire</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Factory C</td>
              <td class="p-3">$1.82/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.32 kg/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">$1.38/kg</td>
              <td class="p-3">Heaviest mesh, Class 3 zinc; best actual value</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Note what happened: Factory B's attractive per-square-meter price evaporates once normalized — they are selling you less steel. Factory C, nominally the most expensive, delivers the most metal per dollar. Now anchor the contract: state theoretical weight (with tolerance, typically minus zero, plus 3%), require a weighbridge ticket or weighed-roll records, and make the balance payment contingent on the weight check. The <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a> automates this normalization and adds inland charges, export fees, and freight so you compare true landed cost, not sticker price.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">The Cluster Buyer's Mantra</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          In Anping, you do not find quality — you specify it, verify it, and pay for exactly what you specified. The cluster's factories will deliver precisely the product you contract for, at a price no other region can match. Buyers who get burned are the ones who bought a price instead of a specification.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How many wire mesh factories are actually in Anping?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Registered manufacturers number in the high hundreds, but the wider cluster — workshops, wire drawers, galvanizers, coaters, and subcontractors across Anping County and neighboring Hengshui, Shijiazhuang, and Cangzhou — is commonly estimated at 20,000+ production entities. The figure that matters is smaller: roughly 100–200 factories capable of consistent export-grade quality with real in-house production. That is the pool worth quoting.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is the cheapest Anping quote automatically a trap?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Not automatically — but treat any quote more than ~15% below the median as a claim to verify, not a price to celebrate. Legitimate cheap quotes come from existing raw-material stock, idle line capacity, or container-load efficiency. Ask the bidder to state wire diameter, zinc coating class, steel grade, and theoretical weight in writing. Factories that answer fully are often safe; evasive answers are your answer.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Should I buy from a trading company or a factory in the cluster?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Direct from factory when your order is a single product category and you can handle communication and inspection — you save the 5–15% trader margin and control quality directly. Use a trading company when you consolidate five product categories into one container, need one counterparty for QC and claims, or are too small for any factory to prioritize. Either way, verify who actually makes the goods with our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">factory directory</a> and a video tour.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I visit the Anping wire mesh cluster?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Fly to Beijing Daxing or Shijiazhuang airport — both about 2–3 hours from Anping by car. The county's annual wire mesh expo (usually autumn) concentrates hundreds of exhibitors in one venue and is the efficient way to survey the cluster in two days. Outside the expo, pre-book factory visits: show up unannounced at the Wire Mesh World market and you will meet mostly trading companies. Combine visits with a third-party audit for any factory you intend to order from.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Does the cluster make sense for small orders?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes, increasingly. Involution has pushed MOQs down: standard mesh is often available from a single roll or pallet, and many factories accept trial orders of 500–2,000 dollars. The caveats: small orders queue behind big ones at busy factories, and LCL sea freight can exceed product value under ~3,000 dollars. For small recurring needs, order a half-year's stock at once and use the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">container loading calculator</a> to check the full-versus-shared container break-even.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

